] [17592186563624 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563624 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563624 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cfed377d-f55c-58f7-a40b-072a8729a585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186562991 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563629 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d992778b-a9e3-5c64-9ebe-4a79b06c6533" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563634 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186563458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563634 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563634 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563634 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186563458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563634 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e93e9559-4f79-5c63-a36b-5702454dc549" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563646 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563646 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563646 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563646 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of $y=\\frac{1}{3}(k-x)(5x+3k)$ is $x=\\frac{1}{10}.$ The value of $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563646 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04b365f6-2d57-5574-aec1-e0a7c1397133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563646 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{10},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{1}{10}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=\\frac{1}{3}(k-x)(5x+3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=k$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{3k}{5}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(k-\\frac{3k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{5k}{5}-\\frac{3k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2k}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{k}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k}{5} & =\\frac{1}{10} \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563653 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563653 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106169" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563653 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563653 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of $y=(x-2k)(x+3k)$ is $x=-2.$ The value of $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563653 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "143580a6-1bb7-5c0f-bc9f-072a4a4b0e12" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563653 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2,$ we will set our result equal to $-2.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(x-2k)(x+3k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=2k$ and ${x}_{2}=-3k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k+(-3k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-k}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-2.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-k}{2} & =-2 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563599 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563654 #edb/ref 1069 "q-311955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563654 #edb/ref 1071 "| **$x$** | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $0$ | $8$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. The parabola opens downward. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "14e0eb8a-dac7-5f73-9945-6c6e124a2b27" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563654 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=2\\,\\text{and}\\,x=8.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{2+8}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563659 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563616 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563659 #edb/ref 1069 "q-311952" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563659 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563659 #edb/ref 1071 "| **$x$** | $-4$ | $-1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $0$ | $-12$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563659 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29e68a84-4d10-52b0-a908-447f3d997c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563659 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=-4\\,\\text{and}\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-4+3}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563685 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563685 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223686" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563685 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563685 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(2x+3)(x-k)$ is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Find the value of $k.$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563685 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c9070fc-7290-592a-a3f4-9617ce57a8a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563685 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{4},$ we will set our result equal to $\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=(2x+3)(x-k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and ${x}_{2}=k.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2}+k)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{2k}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2k-3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{5}{4}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2k-3}{4} & =\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ 2k-3 & =5 \\\\ 2k & =8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563686 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563617 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563686 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563686 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563686 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(x+6)(x-4)$ is $x=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563686 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4cf4852d-d3c2-5b9a-829d-615cf17e0f68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563686 #edb/ref 1074 "From the equation $y=-(x+6)(x-4),$ we can tell that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-6$ and ${x}_{2}=4.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-6+4}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563697 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563697 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563697 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563697 #edb/ref 1071 "The axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=-(3x+4)(6x-k)$ is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ The value of $k=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563697 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "684d163d-4d1e-51cd-8879-d5e5b2f089b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563697 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we will calculate the axis of symmetry in terms of $k.$ Then, since we know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we will set our result equal to $-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nFrom the equation $y=-(3x+4)(6x-k),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{k}{6}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{4}{3}+\\frac{k}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{8}{6}+\\frac{k}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{k-8}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{k-8}{12} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ k-8 & =-4 \\\\ k & =-4+8 \\\\ k & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563701 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563701 #edb/ref 1069 "q-220354" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563701 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563701 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bd/bd46cb7a3139ccb19b7f65bab927ef8c56ba075520262649d6fa196cdab8cc81.png)\n\nThe graph above shows a parabola. The $x$-coordinates of $A$ and $B$ are $-\\frac{16}{5}$ and $\\frac{5}{2},$ respectively.  Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola.\n\nThe axis of symmetry is $x=$\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563701 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e6642fc-4b0f-571e-8c66-e87cc2512b11" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563701 #edb/ref 1074 "We're given that the roots of the parabola are \n ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{16}{5}$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{16}{5}+\\frac{5}{2})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{32}{10}+\\frac{25}{10})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{7}{10})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{7}{20}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563705 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563705 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106153" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563705 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563705 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(x+1)(x+5).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563705 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a1b4ade-6b6f-5b17-86f4-ce0cd6af2275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563705 #edb/ref 1074 "From the equation $y=(x+1)(x+5),$ we can tell that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=-5.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-1)+(-5)}{2} \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563721 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563629 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563721 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84895" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563721 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563721 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ce/ce468433e58a99da31fb305428ff478ddb4c91ec6fa260ab1a951979e83ec63c.png)\n\nFind the axis of symmetry for the parabola sketched above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563721 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a9694bfd-2888-58c6-a0ef-19d3c4d9b6a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563721 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we deduce that the roots are ${x}_{1}=-4$ and ${x}_{2}=3.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-4)+3}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563728 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563574 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563728 #edb/ref 1069 "q-311909" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563728 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563728 #edb/ref 1071 "| **$x$** | $8$ | $10$ | $16$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $18$ | $12$ | $12$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563728 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bac157c9-805d-56d0-9e89-ae7a2f0ad4e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563728 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=10$ and $x=16$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(10)=f(16)=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=10$ and $x=16.$ We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k),$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{10+16}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{2} \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563735 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563549 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563735 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563735 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563735 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(6x-1)(3x+1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563735 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cd5e69ab-4017-5036-a080-1d135b13f5a0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563735 #edb/ref 1074 "From the equation $y=(6x-1)(3x+1),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{6}$ and ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{1}{3})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{6}-\\frac{2}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{6})}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=-\\frac{1}{12}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563739 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563739 #edb/ref 1069 "q-311956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563739 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563739 #edb/ref 1071 "| **$x$** | $-9$ | $-6$ | $-3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $-10$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. The parabola opens downward. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563739 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d2b05f92-b206-55b0-8a81-97fed9a6e7ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563739 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the roots of the parabola are given. They are \n\n$$\nx=-6\\,\\text{and}\\,x=-3.\n$$\n\nIf the coordinates of the vertex are $(h,k),$ then by the average of the roots formula, $h$ is simply the average of the two roots. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{-6+(-3)}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563740 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563541 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563740 #edb/ref 1069 "q-311977" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563740 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563740 #edb/ref 1071 "| **$x$** | $22$ | $25$ | $29$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$f(x)$** | $7$ | $7$ | $1$ |\n\nThe graph of a parabola $y=f(x)$ passes through the points given by their $(x,y)$-coordinates in the table above. What is the $x$-coordinate of the parabola's vertex?\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the vertex is\n{{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563740 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3719257-12a3-5f28-b262-f0a159519b77" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563740 #edb/ref 1074 "Notice that the values of $f(x)$ at the points $x=22$ and $x=25$ are equal.\n\n$$\nf(22)=f(25)=7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parabola's axis of symmetry must be equidistant from the lines $x=22$ and $x=25.$ We can find this by taking the average of these two values.\n\nIf the parabola's vertex is $(h,k),$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h & =\\frac{22+25}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{47}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563746 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563571 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563746 #edb/ref 1069 "q-84903" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563746 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563746 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the axis of symmetry of the parabola $y=(7-5x)(x+1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563746 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "da0e0865-56b2-565d-ac33-deaee1268cf4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563746 #edb/ref 1074 "From the equation $y=(7-5x)(x+1),$ we find that the roots of the parabola are ${x}_{1}=\\frac{7}{5}$ and ${x}_{2}=-1.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{5}-1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{7}{5}-\\frac{5}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{5})}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{5}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563762 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186563526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563762 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563762 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563762 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/6e/6ecca5e6b96cd7e0de0510abf26b3fe80c6f33322460b9bf4448f4b9c3eb8442.png)\n\nFind the axis of symmetry for the parabola sketched above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563762 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8e7577e-52b0-551a-a683-aebcdeb0ba63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186563762 #edb/ref 1074 "From the graph, we deduce that the roots are ${x}_{1}=-1$ and ${x}_{2}=2.$ \n\nTo find the axis of symmetry, we take the average of the roots, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}}{2}=\\frac{(-1)+2}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the axis of symmetry is $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
